course: dictionary
lesson: 10667
title: Unlimited: read the small print
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Unlimited means without any limit: as much or as many as you want. A phone plan with «unlimited data» lets you use as much data as you like. Unlimited can also mean very great, as in «unlimited patience».
explain: Adverts love this word because it sounds exciting, but read the small print. Most «unlimited» deals have hidden rules that you only find if you look for them. Ask yourself: what is the catch?
explain: «A YouTube video promised a secret glitch for unlimited coins, 100% working.» The word unlimited is the promise, but the clues around it are warnings. A secret glitch that a video shares for free, with a claim of 100% working, should make you suspicious. Check whether the promise is true before you trust it.
explain: The comments are a clue too. «The comments said "it works!" from accounts with no pictures and the same words.» Many accounts with no pictures, all writing the same thing, may be fake. A fake comment can make a trick look real, so do not believe something just because others seem to agree.
fact: Unlimited means without any limit, as much or as many as you want.
fact: In maths, an unlimited number has no top limit.
fact: Most «unlimited» deals in real life have hidden rules, so read the small print.
fact: «Unlimited» is often used in adverts because it sounds very appealing.
fact: A promise that sounds too good to be true, with a secret or a glitch, is a clue that you should check carefully.
exercise: «A YouTube video promised a secret glitch for unlimited coins, 100% working.» What does unlimited mean here? | Coins from a shop in town | Few coins, only a small amount | * Without any limit, as many coins as you want | Coins that are fake and made of plastic
exercise: In the same advert, which word is a clue that it may be a trick? | coins | * secret | video | promised
exercise: Ella sees an advert for «unlimited data». What should she do before she believes it? | Share it with everyone she knows | Ask her friend to buy it for her | Nothing, it means exactly what it says | * Read the small print for hidden rules
test: In maths, what does an unlimited number have? | Only zero | A limit of ten | * No top limit | A number with a letter in it
test: «The comments said "it works!" from accounts with no pictures and the same words.» What does this suggest? | Every account always has a picture | The video is definitely true | The comments are from famous people | * The comments may be fake, so be careful
exam: Which is the best clue that an offer may be a trick? | A friend's honest review | A link to the company's own website | A clear price written on the screen | * A secret glitch promised, with no proof
